// BANTU PEOPLES (THE BANTU EXPANSION) — full English content. Same structure and same image slots as the Portuguese version (dados.js).
// Method note: “Bantu” names a family of LANGUAGES (and, by extension, the peoples who speak them), not a “race” and not a state. There is no “Bantu empire”: this is the story of an expansion of languages, farmers, cattle-keepers and ironworkers over thousands of years. Almost everything we know comes from comparative linguistics, archaeology and, more recently, genetics; dates are approximate and much debated. BC/AD.

const visao = [
  { caixa: 'In brief', texto: [
    'The **Bantu peoples** were not a state or an empire: they were the outcome of one of the greatest human expansions in African history. From a homeland that most specialists place in **south-western Cameroon and eastern Nigeria** (the *Grassfields* and the Cross River area), communities speaking related languages, and combining fishing, farming, pottery and, later, **iron**, spread over millennia across equatorial, eastern and southern Africa. Today there are **between about 440 and 680 Bantu languages** (the figure depends on how language is told from dialect), spoken by more than **350 million people**, close to a third of Africa’s population.',
    'The process was **slow, uneven and made of many small movements**, not a single “migration” of one people on the march: some families moved on, some groups stayed, there was intermarriage and exchange with hunter-gatherers and with farmers already settled, languages were adopted and languages were lost. The chronology, the routes and the causes are still **debated** among linguists, archaeologists and geneticists, and this chapter always says when something is a hypothesis. What nobody disputes is the result: Bantu languages are the shared heritage of a large part of Africa, from Cameroon to Kenya and on to the Cape, and the civilizations covered on other pages of this project (**Kongo**, **Great Zimbabwe**, the **Swahili coast**) grew out of Bantu-speaking populations.'
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-mapa-expansao', leg: 'Schematic map of the Bantu expansion; routes and dates are hypotheses and vary from author to author.' },
  { h: 'Where it lay' },
  'There is no fixed “Bantu land”: the historical space is the whole southern half of the continent. The **homeland** (the region where Proto-Bantu, the mother language reconstructed by linguists, was spoken) lies on the border between **south-eastern Nigeria** and **south-western Cameroon**: highland grasslands and forest, with fertile valleys and volcanoes, cut by rivers that run to the Gulf of Guinea and, further south, to the Congo. The exact location is a hypothesis, based on the fact that this is where the oldest and most diverse branches of the family to which Bantu belongs are found.',
  'From there, the map of the expansion has two great masses: the **equatorial rainforest** of the Congo basin (Gabon, Congo, Democratic Republic of the Congo), and the **savannas and miombo woodlands** of eastern and southern Africa, crossed by lakes, rivers and plateaus. There was also a third dimension, time: many regions where Bantu is spoken today were reached only after centuries, and some (coastal Kenya, Natal, the Limpopo valley) only in the early centuries AD.',
  { img: 'ban-camaroes-monte', leg: 'Mount Cameroon, a volcano in south-western Cameroon, in the general region where the Bantu languages originated.' },
  { h: 'When it existed' },
  'According to linguistic estimates, Proto-Bantu began to separate from neighbouring languages **about 5000 to 4000 years ago** (c. 3000 – 2000 BC); the first dispersals and the securest iron come from the 1st millennium BC; and the main expansion runs, broadly, to the 1st millennium AD. The closing limit of this chapter is c. AD 1000, but the “legacy” of course reaches our own day. The dates below are approximate, and several of them (especially the oldest) are hypotheses.',
  { tabela: { cab: ['Phase', 'Approximate dates', 'What marks it'], linhas: [
    ['Origins', 'c. 3000 – 1000 BC', 'Proto-Bantu in south-western Cameroon and eastern Nigeria; fishing, yam, oil palm, pottery; first dispersals (very discreet in the archaeology)'],
    ['Iron and first dispersals', 'c. 1000 BC – 0', 'Iron in West and Central Africa (Nok, as context, and other centres); villages in the forest; Urewe in the Great Lakes (c. 500 BC)'],
    ['Main expansion', 'c. 500 BC – AD 500', 'Savanna corridor, Great Lakes, east coast (Kwale), southern Africa (c. AD 200 – 400); iron and, in many regions, cereals and cattle'],
    ['Consolidation', 'c. AD 500 – 1000', 'Demographic collapse in the forest (c. 400 – 600); regional diversification; chiefdoms and trade with the Indian Ocean; Later Iron Age'],
    ['States and legacy', 'c. AD 1000 onwards', 'Mapungubwe and, with pages of their own, Great Zimbabwe, Kongo and the Swahili city-states; today’s Bantu languages and cultures']
  ] } },
  { img: 'ban-esquema-rotas', leg: 'Simplified diagram of the two great routes of the Bantu expansion, through the equatorial forest and through the savannas of the east and south. Map generated by AI. (Illustrative image generated by AI.)' },
  { h: 'Who were the Bantu peoples?' },
  'The word **Bantu** was proposed in the mid-19th century by the German philologist **Wilhelm Bleek**, who noticed that many languages of southern Africa formed the plural of persons with the prefix *ba-* on a root *-ntu*, “person”: *ba-ntu*, “the people”. It is therefore a **linguistic term**: speaking a Bantu language does not make anyone a member of a “race” or of a single “people”. Bantu speakers have very different histories, ancestries, religions and political systems: from forest hunters who speak Bantu to savanna kings, from coastal fishers to highland cattle-keepers.',
  'In terms of **linguistic kinship**, Bantu is a gigantic subgroup of the **Niger-Congo** family (the largest language family in the world by number of languages), more precisely of the **Benue-Congo** branch, and within it of the **Bantoid** zone, which includes languages still spoken in Cameroon and Nigeria. This means Bantu languages are **cousins** of languages such as Yoruba, Igbo or Efik, with which they share a remote origin, and not of Arabic, Amharic, Hausa or the Khoe and San languages (the “clicks” of some southern Bantu languages, such as Zulu and Xhosa, were borrowed from the latter).',
  { img: 'ban-mapa-niger-congo', leg: 'Map of the Niger-Congo languages; the Bantu subfamily is the branch that stretches from Cameroon to the south of the continent.' },
  { h: 'Why they matter' },
  { lista: [
    '**Africa’s greatest linguistic dispersal:** related languages, from Swahili to Zulu, from Kikongo to Shona, spoken in about 30 countries.',
    '**The spread of iron and farming:** Bantu-speaking communities carried (or adopted and spread) iron, pottery, crops and livestock across vast areas; it was one of the great revolutions in African economy.',
    '**A laboratory for science:** few cases in human history allow **linguistics, archaeology and genetics** to be crossed so well, and let us ask how a language spreads and who carries it.',
    '**The basis of great states:** Kongo, Great Zimbabwe, Mapungubwe, the Great Lakes kingdoms, the Swahili cities and others grew out of Bantu-speaking societies.',
    '**A lesson in rigour:** the subject was often treated with prejudice (see below), and modern study shows how different the real story is: more complex, slower and more peaceful than was imagined.',
    '**A living heritage:** languages, rhythms, proverbs and words that also reached the Americas and Portuguese, above all through the Atlantic slave trade.'
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-aldeia-floresta', leg: 'Village of farmers and fishers in a clearing of the equatorial forest, c. 500 BC; conjectural reconstruction. Illustration generated by AI.' },
  { caixa: 'The Bantu today', texto: 'Bantu languages are spoken across almost all of sub-equatorial Africa, in countries such as the **Democratic Republic of the Congo**, **Angola**, **Mozambique**, **Tanzania**, **Kenya**, **Uganda**, **Zambia**, **Zimbabwe**, **South Africa**, **Gabon** and **Cameroon**, among many others. **Swahili**, **Lingala**, **Zulu**, **Xhosa**, **Shona**, **Kikongo**, **Kimbundu**, **Umbundu**, **Chewa**, **Kinyarwanda** and **Ganda** are only some of the larger languages. Portuguese has many words of Bantu origin (above all from Kimbundu and Kikongo), such as *fubá*, *caçula*, *quitanda* or *quilombo*. The term “Bantu” carries, in certain contexts (especially in South Africa), a heavy historical load; see the “note of caution” at the end.' }
];

const linha = [
  'This timeline follows the expansion from the homeland to the Later Iron Age. There are no kings or dated battles: dates come from **radiocarbon** (mostly charcoal and seeds), **pottery** and **linguistic estimates**, with margins of error of centuries. Where something is a hypothesis or a disputed result, the text says so. The last entries point to civilizations that have pages of their own.',
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 6000 BC and earlier', t: 'Shum Laka and the Niger-Congo languages', x: 'The **Niger-Congo** family is very old (estimates of about 10,000 years exist, debated) and has its most diverse branches in West Africa. At the rock shelter of **Shum Laka**, in the Grassfields of Cameroon, there is human occupation over tens of thousands of years and **pottery** thousands of years old. In 2020, DNA from four children buried there (c. 8000 and c. 3000 years ago) showed that they belonged to a population that is **not ancestral** to today’s Bantu speakers: the geographical cradle of the languages is not, then, the genetic cradle of all who speak them.' },
    { d: 'c. 3000 – 2000 BC (estimate)', t: 'Proto-Bantu emerges', x: 'Language family trees built with statistical methods (**Grollemund** and colleagues, 2015; **Koile** and colleagues, 2022) place the root of the Bantu family about **5000 to 4000 years ago**, in the border zone between **south-eastern Nigeria** and **south-western Cameroon**. The vocabulary linguists can reconstruct shows fishing, canoes, **yam**, **cowpea**, **Bambara groundnut**, **goats**, pottery and the **oil palm**: village societies, with gathering, fishing and still small-scale cultivation.' },
    { d: 'c. 2000 BC (controversial proposal)', t: 'Very early iron?', x: 'Some authors propose iron dates of c. 2000 BC at **Lejja** (Nigeria) and at **Oboui** (Central African Republic). Most archaeologists doubt these dates (because of possible contamination of the charcoal samples and the doubtful link between sample and smelting). The most common consensus accepts secure iron from c. **800 – 500 BC** in several parts of West and Central Africa. Whether it was a local invention or arrived from the north is also debated.' },
    { d: 'c. 1000 – 400 BC (debated)', t: 'Bananas at Nkang?', x: 'At **Nkang**, in central Cameroon, banana **phytoliths** (plant microfossils) have been identified in a layer attributed to this period. If the identification and the dating are right, the banana, a plant of Asian origin, was already in Africa in the 1st millennium BC. But both are **contested**: some think the phytoliths have moved down from more recent layers, and there is no consensus. Either way, the banana later became a central food in much of equatorial Africa and the Great Lakes.' },
    { d: 'c. 600 BC', t: 'Settlers on the forest rivers', x: 'At sites in Cameroon and Gabon communities appear with **pottery**, large blades and polished axes, at least from c. 600 BC, along the rivers that flow down to the Congo and the Atlantic. The settlements are small and scattered, and rainforest archaeology is difficult (acid soils, dense vegetation, few excavations), so **the beginning of the expansion is the least known part**.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-canoa-rio', leg: 'Dugout canoes going down a river of the equatorial forest; conjectural reconstruction. Illustration generated by AI.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 800 – 500 BC', t: 'Iron, and the Nok culture (context)', x: 'In central Nigeria, the **Nok** culture (c. 1500 BC – AD 1; see the **Nok Culture** page) produced the oldest terracotta sculptures of sub-Saharan Africa and knew **iron metallurgy**. It lies several hundred kilometres north of the homeland of the Bantu languages, and the language the Nok spoke is unknown. **There is no proof of a direct link** between the Nok and Bantu speakers; what can be said is that, at the same time and in the same part of Africa, several groups were adopting iron, and that iron began to reach forest communities in this environment.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-nok-cabeca', leg: 'Terracotta sculpture of the Nok culture, Nigeria (c. 900 BC – c. AD 200; the dating of each piece is uncertain). Here only as context.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 600 – 500 BC', t: 'Urewe and the iron of the Great Lakes', x: 'In the **Lake Victoria** region (Tanzania, Uganda, Rwanda, Burundi, eastern Congo) the **Urewe tradition** appears: pottery decorated with dimples and concentric lines, villages of farmers and cattle-keepers, and clay **iron furnaces** shaped like a cone over a basin, sometimes decorated. The Urewe communities spoke an early Bantu language, Proto-Great Lakes Bantu. The tradition lasts until c. AD 500 and, at some sites, much longer.' },
    { d: 'c. 500 BC – AD 500', t: 'The furnaces of Buhaya', x: 'In the **Buhaya** region, west of Lake Victoria (Tanzania), the archaeologists **Peter Schmidt** and **Donald Avery** studied in the 1970s clay furnaces that, in their view, reached high temperatures, with pre-heated air, and produced **carbon steel**. The interpretation is disputed, and some consider it exaggerated, but the whole shows a developed and original metallurgy, long before contact with Europe.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-forno-ferro', leg: 'Iron smelting in a clay furnace, Great Lakes, c. 300 BC; conjectural reconstruction. Illustration generated by AI.' },
  { img: 'ban-bloomery', leg: 'Modern reconstruction of a direct-reduction furnace (“bloomery”); it illustrates the principle of the technique, not a find from this period.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 500 – 0 BC', t: 'The forest opens up: the savanna corridor', x: 'Pollen and lake sediment data suggest that, c. **2500 to 2000 years ago**, the equatorial forest suffered a disturbance, with a strip of savanna opening up (the so-called **Sangha River Interval**, between Cameroon and the Congo). The hypothesis of **Koen Bostoen** and colleagues (2015) is that this strip of savanna made it easier for populations to move south and east. But the most recent genetic and linguistic studies (Koile, 2022; Fortes-Lima, 2024) suggest that the forest was crossed **first**, and the savannas only afterwards. The discussion is open.' },
    { d: 'c. 300 BC – AD 500', t: 'Encounters with hunter-gatherers', x: 'All along the way, Bantu-speaking farmers met **hunter-gatherers** (in the forest, the peoples now called Twa, Aka, Baka and Mbuti; in the savanna, ancestors of Khoe and San groups). In many regions there was coexistence for centuries, exchanges of honey, meat, iron and pottery, intermarriage and the adoption of words. Genetics shows admixture, above all of **women** from local groups with Bantu men, and the languages show loanwords (the clicks of the Nguni languages are the best-known example). The idea that the Bantu “swept away” the hunters is largely a **myth**.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-encontro-cacadores', leg: 'Encounter between Bantu-speaking farmers and hunter-gatherers, c. AD 100; conjectural reconstruction. Illustration generated by AI.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '1st – 4th c. AD', t: 'Zambia, Malawi and the interior', x: 'Iron-and-pottery communities “of the 1st millennium” (of the so-called **Chifumbaze** tradition, in the terminology of the archaeologist David Phillipson) appear in Zambia, Malawi and the lands between the Zambezi and the Limpopo. The site of **Kalambo Falls**, on the border of Zambia and Tanzania, has an archaeological sequence of hundreds of thousands of years, which includes these iron-working farmers.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-kalambo', leg: 'Kalambo Falls, on the border between Zambia and Tanzania; the site has one of the longest archaeological sequences in Africa, including Iron Age levels.' },
  { linha: [
    { d: 'c. 2nd c. AD', t: 'The east coast: Kwale and Matola', x: 'On the coast of Kenya, Tanzania and Mozambique a pottery of **Kwale** and **Matola** type spreads, linked to Bantu-speaking farming and ironworking communities who followed the coastal strip southwards. The advance was fast: according to some studies, **less than a thousand years** were enough for speakers of the eastern branch to reach the south of the continent.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 200 – 400', t: 'The south: Natal and the Limpopo', x: 'Pottery of the Kwale tradition appears at sites in **KwaZulu-Natal** (South Africa) around **AD 200 – 300**, and the **Kalundu** tradition (c. AD 400) extends into the interior. The first iron-working farmers occupy above all wet zones with good soils, near the coast and in river valleys. To say, as was said for decades in South Africa, that the Bantu arrived “at the same time” as white settlers is therefore false: the Bantu presence in the region is more than 1500 years old.' },
    { d: '3rd – 7th c. AD', t: 'The Zimbabwe plateau', x: 'On the plateau between the Zambezi and the Limpopo, communities of the **Gokomere** tradition practise farming, cattle-keeping and iron metallurgy. This is the ground on which, centuries later, Mapungubwe and Great Zimbabwe would arise (see the **Great Zimbabwe** page).' },
    { d: 'c. AD 400 – 600', t: 'Collapse in the equatorial forest', x: 'A 2021 study (**Seidensticker** and colleagues), based on hundreds of radiocarbon dates from western Central Africa, points to a **sharp drop in human activity** in the forest between c. **AD 400 and 600**, followed by some 400 years of minimal occupation, until a recovery c. AD 1000. According to the authors, the first communities of iron-working farmers largely disappeared, and today’s populations would descend from **groups that arrived later**. The causes (epidemic? climate change? both?) are discussed, and the interpretation is much debated.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 500 – 700 (dating debated)', t: 'The Lydenburg Heads', x: 'In 1957, seven hollow **terracotta** heads, about 25 to 38 cm high and linked to a community of iron-working farmers, were found at **Lydenburg** (Mpumalanga, South Africa). They date, broadly, to the 6th–7th century AD. Their function is discussed; one hypothesis is that they were used in initiation rituals. They are among the oldest sculptures of southern Africa and show an art of its own already in the first communities of the south.' },
    { d: '7th – 10th c. AD', t: 'The Later Iron Age', x: 'Pottery and ways of life diversify by region. In many areas **cereals** (sorghum and millet) and **cattle** gain weight, and larger **chiefdoms** appear. In Botswana, **Toutswe** and **Bosutswe** (from c. AD 700) are centres with cattle and stone structures. Trade with the Indian Ocean coast grows: glass beads, cloth and ivory.' },
    { d: '8th – 15th c. AD', t: 'Swahili cities (cross-reference)', x: 'Along the east coast, Bantu-speaking communities in intense contact with Indian Ocean traders form the cities of the **Swahili coast** (Kilwa, Mombasa, Lamu, Zanzibar and others). Swahili is a Bantu language, with many words borrowed from Arabic. It has a page of its own: see **Swahili City-States**.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 1000 – 1300', t: 'K2 and Mapungubwe', x: 'In the Limpopo valley, the village of **K2** and then the hill of **Mapungubwe** (c. 1220 – 1290) reveal a Bantu-speaking elite with gold, ivory and glass beads from the Indian Ocean. Among the best-known pieces is the **golden rhinoceros**, found in 1933. The site is also treated on the **Great Zimbabwe** page.' },
  ] },
  { img: 'ban-mapungubwe', leg: 'Mapungubwe, in the Limpopo valley (South Africa): centre of a Bantu-speaking elite, c. 1220 – 1290 (World Heritage Site, 2003).' },
  { linha: [
    { d: '11th – 15th c. AD', t: 'Great Zimbabwe (cross-reference)', x: 'On the Zimbabwe plateau, a Bantu-speaking society (ancestors of the Shona) raised the great granite walls of Great Zimbabwe. It has a page of its own: see **Great Zimbabwe**.' },
    { d: 'c. AD 1390', t: 'The Kingdom of Kongo (cross-reference)', x: 'In the lower Congo basin, tradition places the founding of a kingdom by Lukeni lua Nimi. It is one of the best-documented Bantu states, in contact with Portugal from c. 1483. It has a page of its own: see **Kingdom of Kongo**.' },
    { d: '16th – 19th c. AD', t: 'History goes on', x: 'After the year 1000, Bantu-speaking societies created kingdoms, empires and cities of every size, from Buganda to the Zulu. The region of **West-Central Africa** was, between the 16th and 19th centuries, the largest source of enslaved Africans sent to the Americas, and Bantu languages and cultures thus reached Brazil, Cuba and other regions. The limit of this chapter is, however, the turn of the millennium.' }
  ] },
  { h: 'Rediscovery' },
  'The idea of “Bantu” as a unit of language was born in the 19th century, when **Wilhelm Bleek** (1857 – 1862) showed the regularity of the languages of southern Africa, and **Carl Meinhof** (1899) gave it a comparative grammar. In the 20th century, **Malcolm Guthrie** (1948; 1967 – 1971) classified the languages into lettered zones (A to S) and reconstructed Proto-Bantu words; **Joseph Greenberg** (1955, 1963) showed that the homeland lay in Cameroon and Nigeria and not, as Guthrie had thought, in the south of the Congo. In 1966 the historian **Roland Oliver** linked the spread of Bantu to the spread of iron. From the 1960s to the 1990s, **Jan Vansina**, **David Phillipson**, **Christopher Ehret** and others merged language and archaeology into a single history of the expansion.',
  'Since 2015, **genetics** and statistical methods have changed the debate: the language trees of **Grollemund** and colleagues (2015) and of **Koile** and colleagues (2022), the ancient DNA of **Shum Laka** (2020), the study by **Seidensticker** and colleagues (2021) on the forest, and the large genetic study by **Fortes-Lima** and colleagues (2024, 1763 people from 147 populations) tightened the chronologies and called several old ideas into question. The history of the expansion is today one of the most active areas of African prehistory.'
];

